            <ArticleTitle>[Pericardial tamponade in rupture of a right coronary artery aneurysm].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>187-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cardiovascular magnetic resonance is a valid and accurate tool for the assessment of structural and functional disorders of the heart. Through combination of morphologic and functional studies, it is possible to form a quick diagnosis with a noninvasive examination. In this case, MR-imaging shows a pericardial effusion and a 5 x 5 cm large, well-circumscribed hematoma with displacement of the right coronary artery, which was also visible with other examination techniques as an unclear intracardial mass. At operation a large aneurysm of the right coronary artery was exposed, in addition to a hemorrhagic pericardial effusion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
